course: dictionary
lesson: 7148
title: Percentages: a part of 100
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A percentage tells you how many parts out of 100. The sign % means "per hundred", so 25% means 25 out of every 100. «A large percentage of the class» means a big share of the pupils.
explain: To find 10% of 50, split 50 into 100 equal parts, which is 0.5 each, and 10 of them make 5. You can also think of 10% as one tenth. Half of anything is 50%, so ½ equals 50%.
explain: Percentages can make small numbers sound scary. «A big percentage can sound scary when the real numbers are tiny: up from 2 to 3 cases is "up 50%".» The rise is 50%, but it is only one more case. Always ask «out of how many?»
fact: Percentage means "out of 100", and the sign for it is %.
fact: 25% means 25 out of every 100.
fact: To find 10% of an amount, find one tenth of it.
fact: «Percentage increase» means how much something has gone up, as a percentage of where it started.
fact: The unemployment rate is the percentage of people looking for work who don't have it; 4% means 4 out of every 100.
exercise: What does 25% mean? | *25 out of every 100 | 250 out of every 100 | 25 out of every 10
exercise: What is 10% of 50? | 500 | 40 | 50 | *5
exercise: Which fraction is the same as 50%? | 1/5 | *1/2 | 1/4 | 1/10
exercise: In «a large percentage of the class», what does percentage mean? | *a part or share of something | a number of pupils exactly | a sum of money | a type of sign for adding
test: Up from 2 to 3 cases is a rise of how many percent? | 10% | 1% | *50% | 100%
test: What sign do we write to show a percentage? | *% | & | $ | #
exam: Why does a rise from 2 to 3 cases sound scary at 50%? | Because 3 is more than 50 | *Because the real numbers are tiny: only one more case | Because 50 is a big number | Because percentages are always wrong
